\section{Introduction}

Helium (He) nanodroplets are fascinating quantum fluid clusters with distinct properties compared to other types of atomic and molecular clusters. The constituent He atoms are loosely bound to one another by extremely weak attractive \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{dispersive }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{London dispersion }\DIFaddend forces and their light mass implies a large zero-point energy, i.e., the emergence of collective quantum behavior. Notably, He nanodroplets evaporatively cool to the ultralow temperature of 0.37~K, where they exhibit microscopic superfluidity~\cite{Grebenev:1998,Hartmann:1996,Tang:2002,Brauer:2013}. \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{Electronic excitations }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{Although considered inert, upon excitation or ionization, He nanodroplets can become a highly reactive environment where numerous interatomic processes can occur~\mbox{%DIFAUXCMD
\cite{Mudrich2014}}\hspace{0pt}%DIFAUXCMD
.
}

\DIFadd{In general, electronic excitations in nanodroplets }\DIFaddend are mostly localized on single He atoms \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{(He$^*$) }\DIFaddend which tend to form void cavities (`bubbles') \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{, }\DIFdelend due to the repulsive interaction between the excited electron and the surrounding He~\cite{Haeften:2001,Closser2010,Kornilov2011}. This leads to a broadening of \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{the }\DIFaddend excited states in absorption spectra~\cite{Joppien1993}, although they largely retain their atomic \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{characteristics}\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{character}\DIFaddend . We will refer to these broadened features as `bands' but \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{have labeled }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{we label }\DIFaddend them with their corresponding atomic electronic configuration. 
\DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{He$_2^*$ excited dimers may form, but the fraction of those directly formed by ultrafast association of an excited He$^*$ atom and a ground state He atom is very small~\mbox{%DIFAUXCMD
\cite{Ziemkiewicz2015,Closser2014}}\hspace{0pt}%DIFAUXCMD
. The main formation mechanism of He$_2^*$ in large He droplets as well as in bulk superfluid He, tunneling into excited vibrational states of He$_2^*$, is much slower ($\sim 15~\mu$s) than any dynamics probed in the present experiment~\mbox{%DIFAUXCMD
\cite{keto1972dynamics,Buchenau:1991}}\hspace{0pt}%DIFAUXCMD
. 
}

\DIFaddend Quasi-free electrons \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{in an excited nanodroplet }\DIFaddend occupy states in a `conduction band' $\gtrsim 1$~eV above the vacuum level~\cite{jortner1965study,Wang}. The corresponding electronic wavefunctions are \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{confined to }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{localized in }\DIFaddend the interstitial spaces between He atoms~\cite{Borghesani:2007}. Autoionization of these highly-excited states then leads to the emission of small charged He clusters He$_n^+$, $n=2,\,3,\dots$ and low-energy electrons~\cite{BuenermannJCP:2012,Peterka2003}.
\DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{Furthermore, the position of the localized excitation in large-scale systems can have an impact on the underlying dynamics. Thus, the size-tunability of He nanodroplets from nanometers, where the majority of atoms is located within the surface region, to micrometers, where the dominant fraction of atoms are mainly located in the droplet bulk, offers the capability to test surface\,}\textit{\DIFdel{vs.}}%DIFAUXCMD
\DIFdel{\,bulk effects~\mbox{%DIFAUXCMD
\cite{Haeften:2011}}\hspace{0pt}%DIFAUXCMD
. 
}\DIFdelend 


%DIF > % Aaron: Should we delete this paragraph ? We don't actually discuss any surface / bulk effects...
%DIF > The position of the localized excitations in large-scale systems can have an impact on the underlying dynamics. Thus, the size-tunability of He nanodroplets from nanometers, where the majority of atoms is located within the surface region, to micrometers, where the dominant fraction of atoms are mainly located in the droplet bulk, offers the capability to test surface\,\textit{vs.}~bulk effects~\cite{Haeften:2011}. 
\DIFaddbegin 


\DIFaddend The precise electronic structure of excited He nanodroplets still remains to be fully resolved~\cite{Closser2010,Ziemkiewicz2015}. In particular, the dynamics of electron localization, relaxation, and atomic rearrangements induced by the electronic excitations are subjects of \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{current }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{ongoing }\DIFaddend research~\cite{Closser2014}. The ultrafast dynamics of excited He nanodroplets has been studied in several femtosecond pump-probe experiments employing both laser-based high-harmonic \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{generation (HHG) }\DIFaddend sources~\cite{Ziemkiewicz2015} and extreme-ultraviolet (XUV) free-electron lasers (FELs)~\cite{Mudrich,asmussen2021unravelling}. Essentially, ultrafast localization of the excitation on single atomic sites was confirmed, followed by the electronic relaxation into metastable states, and the formation of nanoscale bubbles around the excited atoms on the timescale of $\sim 1$~ps. \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{In the first HHG-based experiments where near-infrared probe pulses were used, ejection of He$^*$ excited atoms was observed~\mbox{%DIFAUXCMD
\cite{Kornilov2011}}\hspace{0pt}%DIFAUXCMD
. Subsequent experiments by the same group showed that UV pulses are better suited for probing the full He droplet dynamics and that the ejection of a He$^*$ atom actually is a minor relaxation channel~\mbox{%DIFAUXCMD
\cite{Ziemkiewicz2014,Ziemkiewicz2015}}\hspace{0pt}%DIFAUXCMD
.
}\DIFaddend The lowest excited atomic state of He, \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{1s2s\,$^3S$}\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{$1s2s\,^3S$}\DIFaddend , has only been observed at photon energies $h\nu\gtrsim 23~$eV, where He droplets autoionize and optically inaccessible states can be populated through electron-ion recombination~\cite{VonHaeften1997,asmussen2021unravelling}. Static measurements have shown that even highly excited He droplet states efficiently relax into the \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{lowest-excited 1s2s\,$^{1,\,3}S$ }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{lowest excited $1s2s\,^{1,\,3}S$ }\DIFaddend atomic states~\cite{BenLtaief2019}. Eventually, He$_2^*$ excimers form at the He droplet surface, detaching from the droplets over the course of vibrational relaxation~\cite{Buchenau:1991,VonHaeften1997}. 

In this article, we present an overview of the XUV-pump, UV-probe dynamics of resonantly excited He nanodroplets over a wide range of excitation energies and electron energies. \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{We specifically }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{Subsequently, we }\DIFaddend focus on the electronic relaxation processes occurring in the lowest $1s2p$ and $1s2s$ droplet bands~\cite{Joppien1993}. \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{So far, }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{Our }\DIFaddend previous measurements~\cite{Mudrich,asmussen2021unravelling} have been limited by the spectrometer resolution \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{, however, here, high resolution time-resolved photoelectron spectroscopy allows us }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{and by experimental noise from XUV/UV stray light. Here, with improved resolution and a higher signal-to-noise ratio, we are able }\DIFaddend to observe new features \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{in the relaxation dynamics}\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{due to relaxation in the time-resolved photoelectron spectra}\DIFaddend . In particular, \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{even the }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{when exciting the system to the }\DIFaddend lowest optically allowed droplet states\DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{correlating to the }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{, }\DIFaddend $1s2s\,^1S$ and \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{$^1P$ excited He$^*$ states are found to relax }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{$1s2p\,^1P$, we observe efficient relaxation }\DIFaddend into triplet atomic states, indicating \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{efficient }\DIFdelend droplet-induced spin-relaxation \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{. 
}\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{as well as population of the first excimer state of He$_2^*$. Compared to previous work at higher excitation energies~\mbox{%DIFAUXCMD
\cite{Ziemkiewicz2015,asmussen2021unravelling}}\hspace{0pt}%DIFAUXCMD
, the relaxation process discussed here is simpler since it only involves intra-band relaxation as opposed to a combination of inter- and intra-band relaxations. Thus, with a simpler excitation scheme and improved resolution, we are able to better distinguish weak features in the photoelectron spectrum and to probe their ultrafast dynamics on femtosecond timescales.      
}\DIFaddend 

\section{Experimental methods}

The pump-probe experiment was performed at the Low Density Matter (LDM) endstation~\cite{Lyamayev2013} of the seeded FEL FERMI, in Trieste, Italy~\cite{Allaria2012,Allaria2012a}. The FEL pump pulse\DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{, operated at a repetition rate of 50~Hz, }\DIFaddend was primarily tuned to a photon energy \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{$h\nu_1$ }\DIFaddend near the $1s2p$ droplet resonance ($21.6$~eV)~\cite{Joppien1993} via the seed laser and undulator gaps, yielding a pulse length \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{of approximately 100~fs }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{$t_p\approx 100$~fs (}\DIFaddend full width at half maximum\DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{(}\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{, }\DIFaddend FWHM)~\cite{finetti2017pulse}. The FEL pulse intensity was \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{tuned }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{adjusted }\DIFaddend to limit the effects of multiphoton ionization\DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{~\mbox{%DIFAUXCMD
\cite{Ovcharenko:2014,LaForge:2014,Ovcharenko:2020} }\hspace{0pt}%DIFAUXCMD
}\DIFaddend with typical values of \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{about $1\times10^{10}$}\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{$I_{XUV}\approx 1\times10^{10}$}\DIFaddend ~W/cm$^{2}$, \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{which was determined }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{as derived by the pulse energy measured }\DIFaddend upstream by gas ionization, taking into account the nominal reflectivity of the optical elements in the beam transport system~\cite{Svetina2015}. The UV probe pulse was obtained from a frequency-tripled Ti:Sapphire laser ($h\nu_2\,=\,4.65$~eV) with a pulse intensity of \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{about $1\times10^{11}$}\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{$I_{UV}\approx 1\times10^{11}$}\DIFaddend ~W/cm$^{2}$~\cite{Finetti2017}. A tin filter of 200\,\DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{$\mu$m }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{nm }\DIFaddend thickness was used to suppress contributions from higher order harmonic radiation \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{where }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{whenever }\DIFaddend the pump photon energy \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{$h\nu_1$ }\DIFaddend was tuned to \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{$h\nu_1=21.0$}\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{$21.0$}\DIFaddend ~eV and $21.6$~eV. The cross correlation between the FEL and the probe laser was 200~fs FWHM, measured by resonant (1+1') two-photon ionization of atomic He\DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{,where 1' indicates one-photon absorption by the probe pulse}\DIFaddend ~\cite{Finetti2017}. 

A supersonic jet of He nanodroplets was produced by expansion of high pressure He gas (50~bar) through a pulsed, cryogenically cooled (14~K) Even-Lavie-type nozzle \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{(50~$\mu m$)}\DIFaddend . From the expansion conditions (backing pressure and nozzle temperature), the mean \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{cluster }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{droplet }\DIFaddend size was estimated to \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{be about $10^5$ He atoms }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{$N_\mathrm{He}\approx 10^5$ He atoms per droplet}\DIFaddend ~\cite{Toennies2004a,Gomez:2011}. \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{After expansion, the nanodroplet beam passed through a 1~mm skimmer and traversed approximately 90~cm to the interaction region. }\DIFaddend The nanodroplet beam was perpendicularly crossed by the FEL and UV beams at the center of a high resolution ($E/\Delta E\approx 50$) magnetic bottle electron spectrometer~\cite{Squibb2018}. 

