When a He nanodroplet is resonantly excited by an intense XUV pulse\DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{as provided by FELs}\DIFdelend , a wide variety of dynamic processes \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{opens up}\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{is initiated}\DIFaddend , including internal relaxation, interatomic energy transfer, and multiphoton ionization. To probe the dynamics, we use a time-delayed UV laser \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{with a photon energy $h\nu_2=4.65$~eV, which can directly ionize }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{pulse which directly ionizes }\DIFaddend the excited nanodroplets \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{such that, depending on the delay, one can directly measure the timescales of the processes mentioned above}\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{and we measure the energy of emitted electrons. In this way, we track the dynamics of the various processes as a function of the pump-probe delay}\DIFaddend . Figure~\ref{fig:full_spec_21d0eV}~a) shows \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{a }\DIFdelend representative raw electron kinetic energy \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{distribution }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{distributions }\DIFaddend measured for He nanodroplets that are resonantly excited into the lowest optically allowed droplet state $1s2s\,^1S$ at $h\nu_1=21.0$~eV \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{and for a few different }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{for selected }\DIFaddend pump-probe \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{time }\DIFdelend delays. Fig.~\ref{fig:full_spec_21d0eV}~b) shows \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{a map of }\DIFaddend the electron kinetic energy distributions (\DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{x-axis}\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{$x$-axis}\DIFaddend ) as a function of the pump-probe delay time \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{.
}\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{($y$-axis). To better understand the spectral features, Fig.~\ref{fig:full_spec_21d0eV}~c) shows an energy-level diagram of He atoms (horizontal black lines) and He droplets (gray shaded areas). The vertical arrows depict photo-excitation (red) and photo-ionization steps (black). The horizontal pink arrow indicates autoionization of pairs of excited He atoms in one droplet which is discussed in detail below.
}\DIFaddend 

Overall, numerous dynamic features can be observed in the spectrum \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{at different kinetic energies}\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{in different ranges of the electron energy}\DIFaddend . At low electron \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{kinetic }\DIFdelend energies $\lesssim 2~$eV, we observe prominent features shifting to \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{lower kinetic }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{even lower }\DIFaddend energies as the delay increases. These are generated by 1+1' photoionization through singly excited states of the droplets\DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{, where 1' indicates one-photon absorption by the probe pulse}\DIFdelend . The shifting of the peaks reflects the relaxation of the excited states into low-lying, metastable states, as previously studied for the $1s2p$ band~\cite{Mudrich} and the $1s3p$ band~\cite{asmussen2021unravelling}. Additionally, we observe a similar feature at slightly higher kinetic energies around 5~eV, which is due to the excited state absorbing two UV photons from the probe pulse resulting in 1+2' `above-threshold ionization' (ATI) in the excited system. \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{A second faint replica of the photoionization feature is visible around 10~eV indicating a weak contribution to ATI by three UV photons from the probe pulse. ATI }\DIFdelend %DIF > A second faint replica of the photoionization feature is visible around 10~eV indicating a weak contribution to ATI by three UV photons from the probe pulse. 
\DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{ATI }\DIFaddend of multiply excited He nanodroplets by near-infrared or visible probe pulses has recently been found to be strongly enhanced compared to isolated excited He atoms, pointing at a collective coupling effect~\cite{michiels2021enhancement}. 
At electron energies around 3~eV, there is a time-independent peak, which we attribute to the \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{ionization }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{photoionization }\DIFaddend of background nitrogen \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{molecules by the probe pulse }\DIFaddend through a 3'+1' REMPI scheme. Note that the peak cancels out when the background gas spectrum is subtracted\DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{, therefore, }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{; Therefore, }\DIFaddend it is not \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{associated with }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{related to }\DIFaddend the He nanodroplet \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{molecular }\DIFdelend beam.

The feature at electron \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{kinetic }\DIFdelend energies around 16~eV is due to autoionization of pairs of excited He atoms in one droplet according to a process known as \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{interatomic Coulombic decay (ICD)}\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{ICD}\DIFaddend ~\cite{Cederbaum:1997,Kuleff:2010}. For this particular case, ICD occurs via the process $\mathrm{He}^* + \mathrm{He}^* \rightarrow \mathrm{He} + \mathrm{He}^+ + e_\mathrm{ICD}$ which manifests itself in the emission of electrons $e_\mathrm{ICD}$ with a characteristic energy. \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{For the given experimental parameters, we estimate the number of resonantly excited He$^*$ atoms per He droplet to $N_*=N_\mathrm{He}\sigma_{XUV} t_p I_{XUV}/h\nu_1 \approx 700$, where $\sigma_{XUV}\approx 25~$Mbarn~\mbox{%DIFAUXCMD
\cite{Ovcharenko:2014,Ovcharenko:2020}}\hspace{0pt}%DIFAUXCMD
. This leads to an estimated number of photoelectrons emitted out of the lowest-lying He excited states $N_e^{PI} = N_*\sigma_{UV} t_p I_{UV}/h\nu_2 \approx 100$, given that the photoionization cross sections of these states range from $\sigma_{UV}=4$ to $7~$Mbarn~\mbox{%DIFAUXCMD
\cite{chang1995effect}}\hspace{0pt}%DIFAUXCMD
. The number of electrons created by ICD can be estimated based on the previously measured ICD efficiency, $p_{ICD}\approx 30$~\%, for a droplet excitation rate of $N_*/N_\mathrm{He} \approx 0.7~$\%~\mbox{%DIFAUXCMD
\cite{laforge2020time}}\hspace{0pt}%DIFAUXCMD
, yielding $N_e^{ICD} = N_* \times p_{ICD}/2 \approx 100$. The factor $1/2$ accounts for the fact that it takes a pair of He$^*$ atoms to create an e$_ICD^-$. Thus, we expect to observe roughly an equal amount or photoelectrons and ICD electrons, in agreement with the experimental finding (Fig.~\ref{fig:full_spec_21d0eV}).
}

\DIFaddend At positive pump-probe delays this ICD process is partly quenched because the probe pulse depletes the population of $\mathrm{He}^*$ excited states prior to their decay. From the rise of the ICD signal at delays \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{$\lesssim 1$}\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{$\gtrsim 0.2$}\DIFaddend ~ps we \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{infer }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{previously inferred }\DIFaddend an ICD lifetime on the order of a few hundred fs~\cite{laforge2020time}. \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{We observed only a weak dependence of the ICD lifetime on the degree of He droplet excitation which is controlled by the photon flux $I_{XUV}/h\nu_1$ and the droplet size $N_\mathrm{He}$. This finding was rationalized by a model in which the bubbles forming around $\mathrm{He}^*$ excitations can merge, thereby accelerating two nearby $\mathrm{He}^*$ atoms toward each other~\mbox{%DIFAUXCMD
\cite{laforge2020time}}\hspace{0pt}%DIFAUXCMD
. Thus, the detected ICD lifetime is mainly determined by the quantum fluid dynamics of the merging bubbles, rather than by the distance-dependent ICD rate for a bare pair of $\mathrm{He}^*$ atoms.
}\DIFaddend When the He nanodroplet is strongly excited with more intense FEL pulses, it can spontaneously evolve into a nanoplasma state by `collective autoionization' (not shown in these spectra)~\cite{Ovcharenko:2014,LaForge:2014,Ovcharenko:2020}. In that case the electron spectrum is dominated by a distribution of low-energy electrons due to thermal emission.

For the remainder of this article, we will specifically focus on the relaxation dynamics of \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{singly excited states}\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{resonantly excited droplets}\DIFaddend , which are observed \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{at low electron kinetic energies }\DIFdelend via 1+1' photoionization \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{at low electron energies}\DIFaddend . These measurements were performed with a high-resolution magnetic bottle spectrometer enabling us to distinguish new features, which \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{were previously unobserved due to the improved spectral resolution compared to previous work}\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{previously remained unobserved}\DIFaddend ~\cite{Mudrich}. Fig.~\ref{fig:21d6eV+21d0eV} shows the electron spectra \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{($y$-axis) }\DIFaddend as a function of pump-probe delay \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{($x$-axis) }\DIFaddend for two photon energies, $h\nu_1 = 21.0$~eV and 21.6~eV in a) and c), respectively.
The \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{zero-point }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{zero }\DIFaddend of the pump-probe delay, $t_0$, is determined from the falling edge of the integrated ICD signal as it is quenched by the probe pulse.
\DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{Additionally, static electron spectra are given in }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{Fig.~\ref{fig:21d6eV+21d0eV}e) shows the absorption spectrum of He droplets recorded by Joppien }\textit{\DIFadd{et al.}}\DIFadd{~\mbox{%DIFAUXCMD
\cite{Joppien1993} }\hspace{0pt}%DIFAUXCMD
by means of fluorescence detection along with He$^*$ energy levels and arrows indicating the relaxation pathways following excitation. The two pump photon energies, $h\nu_1 = 21.0$~eV and $21.6$~eV, correspond to excitation into the low-energy shoulder on the main absorption band and to the maximum of this absorption band, respectively. The droplet excitation at $h\nu_1 = 21.0$~eV correlates with the $1s2s\,^{1}S$ atomic state~\mbox{%DIFAUXCMD
\cite{Joppien1993,Closser2010}}\hspace{0pt}%DIFAUXCMD
, which is inaccessible by electric dipole transition in the atom. It is more likely to occur near the surface of the droplet where the inversion symmetry is broken~\mbox{%DIFAUXCMD
\cite{Mudrich}}\hspace{0pt}%DIFAUXCMD
. Excitation into the main absorption peak ($h\nu_1 = 21.6$~eV) can mostly be associated with the $1s2p\,^{1}P$ atomic state~\mbox{%DIFAUXCMD
\cite{Joppien1993,Closser2010}}\hspace{0pt}%DIFAUXCMD
. 
Additionally, Figs.~\ref{fig:21d6eV+21d0eV} }\DIFaddend b) and d) \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{display electron spectra }\DIFaddend at longer delays when the \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{fast }\DIFaddend relaxation dynamics have subsided. For these spectra, as well as the results in Fig.~\ref{fig:24d6eV}, we have \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{applied a background subtraction }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{subtracted the background }\DIFaddend to better observe the \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{relaxation }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{dynamic }\DIFaddend features. Specifically, we subtract, for each time delay, the electron spectrum produced by the FEL pulse alone, \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{i.\,e. }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \textit{\DIFadd{i.\,e.}} \DIFaddend in the absence of the UV pulse. 

We observe some similar features for the two photon energies. Near zero delay, the electron energy is at its highest and matches the combined photon energies of the XUV-pump and UV-probe pulses reduced by the ionization energy of He, $h\nu_1 + h\nu_2 - IP_\mathrm{He} = 1.1$~eV for $h\nu_1 = 21.0$~eV (white dashed line in Fig.~\ref{fig:21d6eV+21d0eV} a) and \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{and }\DIFdelend 1.7~eV for $h\nu_1 = 21.6$~eV (white dashed line in c). For \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{finite }\DIFdelend positive delays we observe a fast relaxation to lower energies. For $h\nu_1 = 21.0$~eV, the relaxation is so fast that it appears as an almost vertical band, meaning relaxation proceeds within the experimental time resolution (200~fs). For $h\nu_1 = 21.6$~eV, the relaxation is slower resulting in a clearly visible shift from 1.7 to 1.3~eV over about 300~fs. This shift is attributed to intraband electronic relaxation towards the lower edge of the droplet band correlating to the \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{1s2p$\,^1P$ }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{$1s2p\,^1P$ }\DIFaddend state of He. Interestingly, we see a significant narrowing of this band at delays $\gtrsim 200$~fs converging toward a \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{narrow peak which }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{sharp peak whose position }\DIFaddend would match the \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{electron energy of the }\DIFdelend $1s2p\,^1P$ He atomic state, see Fig.~\ref{fig:21d6eV+21d0eV}~d). Simultaneously, new sharp features appear at lower electron kinetic energies. The most prominent one is an intense, nearly horizontal \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{static }\DIFdelend band at electron kinetic energy $eKE\approx 0.6$~eV in Fig.~\ref{fig:21d6eV+21d0eV}~c). It was observed in our previous work~\cite{Mudrich} and was attributed to relaxation into the $1s2s\,^{1}S$ droplet state. This relaxation is likely mediated by a crossing of potential energy curves of the He$_2^*$ excimer~\cite{fiedler2014interaction}. It is accompanied by the formation of a void bubble around the localized He$^*$ excitation~\cite{VonHaeften2002}. At later times, this bubble migrates to the droplet surface and \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{is released as }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{releases }\DIFaddend an excited He$^*$ atom or a He$_2^*$ excimer \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{which either remains weakly bound to the droplet surface or fully detaches }\DIFaddend into the vacuum~\cite{Buchenau:1991}. These species have previously been detected by static fluorescence measurements~\cite{VonHaeften1997}. 

Besides the $1s2\,s^{1}S$ atomic state, additional features are clearly visible, which were not observed in our prior work~\cite{Mudrich}. 
\DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{There, we concluded that the relaxation resulted either in the ejection of the aforementioned free excited He$^*$ or the formation of a He$^*$ that remains weakly bound at the He nanodroplet surface. 
