To gain a better understanding of the origin of these features \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{, we rescaled }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{we offset }\DIFaddend the vertical right \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{axis }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{axes }\DIFaddend in Figs.~\ref{fig:21d6eV+21d0eV}~a) and c) to correspond to the excitation energy of the system. In this way, we can \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{attribute }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{visually assign }\DIFaddend the two horizontal bands at higher kinetic energies to the $1s2p\,^3P$ and $1s2p\,^1P$ atomic states, respectively. \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{It should be noted }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{Note }\DIFaddend that in a different experiment where we studied the relaxation dynamics in He nanodroplets excited to the $1s3p$ band~\cite{asmussen2021unravelling}, we did observe a shoulder on the $1s2s\,^1S$ atomic peak, which we now \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{attribute }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{fully resolve and attribute the peak }\DIFaddend to the $1s2p\,^3P$ atomic state. For the static measurements, shown in Figs.~\ref{fig:21d6eV+21d0eV}~b) and d), 
\DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{vertical }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{horizontal }\DIFaddend lines are added to \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{indicate }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{visualize }\DIFaddend the different excited states. These peaks are most prominent for $h\nu_1 = 21.6$~eV. For 21.0~eV, the lower intensity of the $1s2p\,^1P$ atomic state could be due to its \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{nominal }\DIFaddend excitation energy being slightly higher than the FEL photon energy. 

We additionally observe another \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{distribution }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{feature }\DIFaddend at lower kinetic energy, which we attribute to \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{an excimer }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{the $^{3}\Sigma^{+}_{u}$ }\DIFaddend state of the He$^*_2$ \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{dimer, $^{3}\Sigma^{+}_{u}$, }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{excimer }\DIFaddend photoionized into the lowest vibrational state $v=0$ of the He$_2^+$ dimer ion. \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{Photoionization }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{For photoionization }\DIFaddend into the first excited vibrational state $v=1$ of He$_2^+$ \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{is expected at }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{we expect the electron energy to be }\DIFaddend $eKE\approx 0.17~$eV, which roughly matches the position of the asymmetric feature peaked around $eKE=0.1~$eV in the electron spectrum at $h\nu_1=21.6~$eV \DIFdelbegin \DIFdel{. }\DIFdelend \DIFaddbegin \DIFadd{excitation. This feature may also have contributions from autoionization of superexcited He droplets~\mbox{%DIFAUXCMD
\cite{Peterka:2003}}\hspace{0pt}%DIFAUXCMD
; Quasi-bound states $\lesssim 1.1$~eV above the ionization energy of atomic He, $E_i=24.59$~eV, or states bound by $\lesssim 1.6$~eV below $E_i$, populated by `re-excitation' out of the dark state $1s2s\,^3S$, can decay by emission of low-energy electrons~\mbox{%DIFAUXCMD
