\textbf{Remark:} In this example, the insurer obtains $T$ by training a neural network. Nonetheless, there are no constraints on how the insurer constructs $T$. In principle, $\tilde{X}_i$ could be engineered features that are learned through any supervised or unsupervised method, or $\tilde{X}_i$ could be privatized features designed specifically for the purpose of secure data transmission.


% \vspace{-5pt}
\subsection{Pricing under Privatized Sensitive Attributes with Known Noise Rates}
% \vspace{-5pt}
\label{sec:4.2}

In this section, we investigate a scenario where the true sensitive attributes are not directly observable by the TTP. Instead, the TTP only has access to their privatized versions. This situation often occurs in practice when privacy-preserving mechanisms are employed during data collection or transmission stages (refer to Section \ref{sec:1} for detailed discussions). The central inquiry revolves around how the TTP can obtain a fair price, which entails minimization of Eq. (\ref{eq:TTPpopLoss}), without direct access to $D$.

To address this challenge, we employ the concept of local differential privacy (LDP) in our framework. Let $S$ denote the privatized sensitive attributes. The $\epsilon$-LDP mechanism $Q$ is defined as:
\begin{definition}
\label{def:pm}
\[
\max_{s,d,d'}\frac{Q(S = d | d)}{Q(S = s | d')} \le e^{\epsilon}.
\]
Under the randomized response mechanism in \cite{Warner_1965,kairouz2015extremal}, one has:
\[
Q(s|d)=
    \begin{cases}
    \frac{e^{\epsilon}}{|\cD|-1+e^{\epsilon}}:=\pi, \text{if $s = d$}\\
    \frac{1}{|\cD|-1+e^{\epsilon}}:=\bar{\pi}, \text{if $s \ne d$},
    \end{cases}
\]
where $|\cD|$ is the cardinality of $\cD$ and $s$ is sampled from $Q(\cdot | d)$ independently from $X$, $Y$.
\end{definition}
The primary advantage of employing LDP is that the data collector cannot definitely ascertain the true value of sensitive attributes, irrespective of the accuracy of the information provided for any observation in the dataset \citep{mozannar2020fair}. Consequently, any model trained on this dataset preserves differential privacy with respect to the sensitive attributes.

Similar to the setup in Section \ref{sec:4.1}, the insurer observes $\{X_i, Y_i\}_{i=1}^n$, provides a transformation $T$, and passes $\{\tilde{X}_i, Y_i\}_{i=1}^n$ to the TTP. The TTP is to estimate $\mu(X,D)$ by combining the data from the insurer with privatized sensitive attributes $S_i$. Lemma \ref{lemma:Risk-LDP} establishes a population equivalent risk under privacy mechanism $Q(s_i|d_i)$ and Theorem \ref{theorem:GEB} provides the associated statistical guarantees.

\begin{lemma} Given the privacy parameter $\epsilon$, minimizing the risk (Risk-LDP) defined by Eq. (\ref{eq:Risk-LDP}) under $\epsilon$-LDP w.r.t. privatized sensitive attributes $S$ is equivalent to minimizing Eq. (\ref{eq:TTPpopLoss}) w.r.t. true sensitive attributes $D$ at the population level:
\label{lemma:Risk-LDP}
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:Risk-LDP}
    \cR^{LDP}(f_1, \ldots, f_{|\cD|}) = \sum_{k=1}^{|\cD|} \sum_{j=1}^{|\cD|} \left( \boldsymbol{\Pi}_{kj}^{-1} \Ex_{Y, \tilde{X} \mid S = j} \left[ L \bigr(f_k(\tilde{X}),Y \bigr) \right] \cdot \sum_{l = 1}^{|\cD|} \boldsymbol{T}_{kl}^{-1} \bbP(S = l) \right),
\end{equation}
where $\boldsymbol{\Pi}^{-1}$ and $\boldsymbol{T}^{-1}$ are $|\cD| \times |\cD|$ row-stochastic matrics.
\end{lemma}
Empirically, for a given policyholder $i$, the TTP computes $\hat{h}^*(\tilde{X}_i)$ using learned $\{\hat{f}_k(\tilde{X}_i)\}_{k=1}^{|\cD|}$, and returns $\hat{h}^*(\tilde{X}_i)$ and $\hat{\mu}(\tilde{X}_i,D=k)$ for $k=1,\ldots,|\cD|$ to the insurer. We also summarize the above procedure (MPTP-LDP) in an algorithmic manner (see Appendix \ref{app:deferred_alg}). 

\textbf{Remark:} The use of group-specific score functions enables straightforward construction of an equivalent risk for Eq. (\ref{eq:TTPpopLoss}) using only $S_i$. It is crucial not to view it as a limitation of our approach. As discussed in Section 2,  achieving  a closed-form equivalence is not always feasible with a conventional score function $f(\tilde{X},D)$. Existing methods tackling similar challenges often rely on surrogate risks or confine themselves to specific loss functions \citep{li2016learning}.

\begin{theorem} For any $\delta \in (0,\frac{1}{2})$, $C_1 = \frac{\pi + |\cD| - 2}{|\cD|\pi - 1}$, denote $VC(\cF)$ as the VC-dimension of the hypothesis class $\cF$, and $K$ be some constant that depends on $VC(\cF)$. Let $f = \{f_k\}_{k=1}^{|\cD|}$ where $f_k \in \cF$ and let $L: Y \times Y \to \bbR_+$ be a loss function bounded by some constant $M$. Denote $k^* \leftarrow \underset{k}{\arg\max} |\hat{\cR}^{LDP}(f_k)\hat{\bbP}(D = k) - \cR^{LDP}(f_k)\bbP(D = k)|$. If $n \ge \frac{8 \ln{(\frac{|\cD|}{\delta})}}{\min_k \bbP(S = k)}$, then with probability $1 - 2\delta$:
\label{theorem:GEB}
\[
\hat{\cR}^{LDP}(f) \le \cR(f^*) + K\sqrt{\frac{VC(\cF) + \ln{( \frac{\delta}{2})}}{2n}} \frac{2C_1 M|\cD|}{\bbP(S = k^*)}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\textbf{Remark:} The bound grows with $|\cD|$. However, in insurance practice, $|\cD|$ is small in most cases. When $|\cD|$ is large, categorical embedding (see \cite{doi:10.1080/10920277.2022.2123361}) can be applied if regulation permits.

% \vspace{-5pt}
\subsection{Pricing under Privatized Sensitive Attributes with Unknown Noise Rates}
% \vspace{-5pt}
\label{sec:4.3}

This section expands upon the discussion in Section \ref{sec:4.2} to address scenarios where the noise rate of the privacy mechanism is not known a priori. It is essential to note that constructing a population-equivalent risk requires knowledge of $\pi, \bar{\pi}$. However, obtaining such information often proves challenging in practice, particularly when the sensitive attributes are subject to measurement errors (refer to Section \ref{sec:1} for detailed discussions). Within our multi-party framework, we consider a setup akin to that of Section \ref{sec:4.2}, with the key distinction being that the TTP lacks knowledge of the true conditional probabilities $\pi$ and $\bar{\pi}$ for the given privacy mechanism $Q(s_i | d_i)$. To tackle this obstacle, we propose a methodology wherein the TTP first estimates $\pi$ and $\bar{\pi}$ from the data and then uses these estimates to construct the population-equivalent risk, following the approach outlined in Section \ref{sec:4.2}. We summarize the estimation procedure for $\pi$ and $\bar{\pi}$ in Lemma \ref{lemma:NE} and delineate the underlying assumptions that underpin the establishment of statistical guarantees in Theorem \ref{theorem:GEB-NE}.
\begin{lemma} 
\label{lemma:NE}    
Consider $\epsilon$-LDP setting with $\pi \in (\frac{1}{|\cD|}, 1]$ and $\bar{\pi} \in [0, \frac{1}{|\cD|})$. For some transformation of $X$, denoted by $X^* = \tilde{T}(X)$, assume there exists at least one anchor point $X_{\text{anchor}}^*$ in the dataset s.t. $\bbP(D = j^*|X_{\text{anchor}}^*) = 1$ for some $j^* \in [|\cD|]$. Then $\pi = \bbP(S = j^* |X_{\text{anchor}}^*)$. Empirically, for a dataset with $n$ observation, let $\boldsymbol{\eta}_{j^*}^n(X^*) = \left( \hat{\bbP}(S = j^*|X_1^*), \ldots, \hat{\bbP}(S = j^*|X_n^*)\right)$, then $\hat{\pi} = \left\| \boldsymbol{\eta}_{j^*}^n(X^*) \right\|_{\infty}$.
\end{lemma}
% \vspace{-5pt}
Besides Lemma \ref{lemma:NE}, we introduce the assumptions and procedure to establish Theorem \ref{theorem:GEB-NE} in the following (A more detailed discussion is in Appendix \ref{app:deferred_disc}):

\label{procedure:C_1}
\textbf{Step 1: Grouping:} we evenly divide $\{ X_i^*, S_i \}_{i=1}^n,$ into $n_1$ groups, with $m=\frac{n}{n_1}$ samples each. \textbf{Step 2: Estimating within groups:}  for any $k\in [n_1],$ within each group $\{ X_{k,j}^*, S_{k,j}\}_{j=1}^{m}$, we then derive an $m$-dimension vector $\boldsymbol{\eta}_{j^*,k}^m(X_{k,\cdot}^*) = \bigr( \hat{\bbP}_k(S = j^*|X_{k,1}^*), \ldots, \hat{\bbP}_k(S = j^*|X_{k,m}^*) \bigr)$ and $\hat{\pi}_k=\| \boldsymbol{\eta}_{j^*,k}^m(X_{k,\cdot}^*) \|_{\infty}$, as defined in Lemma \ref{lemma:NE}. Then, by a simple plug in to get $\hat{C}_{1,k}=\frac{\hat{\pi}_k+|\cD|-2}{|\cD|\hat{\pi}_k-1}$. \textbf{Step 3: Averaging:} we then estimate $C_1$ using $\hat{C}_1$, computed as $\hat{C}_1=\frac{1}{n_1}\sum_{k=1}^{n_1}\hat{C}_{1,k}, \hat{C}_{1,k}, k\in[n_1]$. 

Next, we state two assumptions used to derive Theorem \ref{theorem:GEB-NE} (noise rate is estimated from the data).
\label{assumption:A}
\textbf{Assumption A:}\ (Sub-exponentiality) For all $k \in [n_1]$, define $\hat{g}_k(X^*)=\hat{\bbP}_k(S=j^* | X^*)$ There exists a constant $M_{g}>0,$ such that $\left \|\hat{C}_{1,k} \right\|_{\psi_1}= \left\| \min_{i\in[m]}\frac{\hat{g}_k(X_{k,i}^*)+|\cD|-2}{|\cD|\hat{g}_k(X_{k,i}^*)-1} \right\|_{\psi_1} \leq M_g$ for all $k\in[n_1],$ where $\|\cdot\|_{\psi_1}$ is the sub-exponential norm: $\|X\|_{\psi_1}=\inf \{t>0 | \Ex [e^{X/t}] \le 2\}$. 

\label{assumption:B}
\textbf{Assumption B:}\ (Nearly Unbiasedness) For all $k\in[n_1]$, $\hat{C}_{1,k}$ is a ``nearly" unbiased estimator of $C_1$, namely $\left|\Ex[\hat{C}_{1,k}]-C_1\right|<\theta$ for all $k\in[n_1]$, where $\theta>0$.

With the above procedure and assumptions, we derive the following theorem:   
\begin{theorem}
\label{theorem:GEB-NE}
For any $\delta \in (0,\frac{1}{3})$, $C_1 = \frac{\pi + |\cD| - 2}{|\cD|\pi - 1}$, denote $VC(\cF)$ as the VC-dimension of the hypothesis class $\cF$, and $K$ be some constant that depends on $VC(\cF)$. If Assumption A, B, and Lemma \ref{lemma:NE} hold, let $f = \{f_k\}_{k=1}^{|\cD|}$ where $f_k \in \cF$ and let $L: Y \times Y \to \bbR_+$ be a loss function bounded by some constant $M$. Denote $k^* \leftarrow \underset{k}{\arg\max} |\hat{\cR}^{LDP}(f_k)\hat{\bbP}(D = k) - \cR^{LDP}(f_k)\bbP(D = k)|$, if $n \ge \frac{8 \ln{(\frac{|\cD|}{\delta})}}{\min_k \bbP(S = k)}$, $n_1\ge \frac{1}{c(\Tilde{\epsilon}-\theta)^2}(M_g+\frac{C_1+\theta}{\ln 2})^2 \ln(\frac{2}{\delta})$, and $M_g+\frac{C_1+\theta}{\ln 2}>\Tilde{\epsilon}>\theta$,  where $c$ is an absolute constant, then with probability $1 - 3\delta$:
\[
\hat{\cR}^{LDP}(f) \le \cR(f^*) + K\sqrt{\frac{VC(\cF) + \ln{( \frac{\delta}{2})}}{2n}} \frac{2(C_1+\Tilde{\epsilon}) M|\cD|}{\bbP(S = k^*)}.  
\]
\end{theorem}

\textbf{Remark 1:} $\hat{C}_1$ is not explicitly shown in the bound, but it is a vital element that connects assumptions and estimation procedure. Its randomness is absorbed in $\tilde{\epsilon}$ (see proof in Appendix \ref{proof:theoremGEB-NE}).

\textbf{Remark 2:} As $n_1$ increases, $\hat{C}_1$ is more accurate, as it is the average of $n_1$ independent variables, resulting in a tighter bound. However, blindly choosing a large $n_1$ is not recommended, since Assumption A will not hold if $m = \frac{n}{n_1}$ is too small. Some light tuning may help select $n_1$ in practice.

\textbf{Remark 3:} Generally speaking, the bound is more adversely affected by the underestimation of $\pi$. Note that the parameter that significantly influences in the error bound is $\frac{1}{\pi - \frac{1}{|\cD|}}$. Hence, when $\pi$ is close to $\frac{1}{|\cD|}$, an underestimation of $\pi$ can be far more detrimental than an overestimation (especially for $\hat{\pi} \le \frac{1}{|\cD|}$). Further, we provide an empirical study on the effect of estimation error in Section \ref{sec:5.2}.


% \vspace{-5pt}
\section{Experiments \& Result}
% \vspace{-5pt}
\label{sec:5}

We first show our main results in a carefully designed toy dataset and then evaluate the performance of our proposed method using two real-world datasets, demonstrating that the experiment results on both synthetic and real data are in support of our theories. Building on these findings, we further provide practical guidelines for implementing our method under various conditions.

\subsection{Synthetic Data}
\label{sec:5.0}

The toy dataset contains $5000$ observations. The response variable $Y$ is healthcare claim cost. There are two non-sensitive attributes $X$, namely age and smoking status. The age variable $X_{\text{age}}$ takes integer values between $18$ and $80$, and the smoking status variable $X_{\text{smoke}}$ is binary (i.e., smoker, non-smoker). The sensitive attribute gender $D$ is a binary variable (i.e., male, female). The privatized sensitive attribute $S$ is generated using Definition \ref{def:pm} with the randomized response mechanism. The true data-generating process is given by:
\[
\begin{aligned}
    Y =& 100 + 4\times X_{\text{age}} \\
    &+ 100 \times \boldsymbol{1}\{X_{\text{smoker}} = \text{smoker}\} \\
    &+ 120 \times \boldsymbol{1}\{D = \text{female}\} \\ 
    &+ 200 \times \boldsymbol{1}\{(X_{\text{age}} \in [20, 40]) \cap (D = \text{female})\} + \epsilon,
\end{aligned}
\]
where $\epsilon \sim N(0,40^2)$. 

We consider the following probabilities and dependency structure:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\bbP(X_{\text{smoker}} = \text{smoker}) = 0.3$, $\bbP(D = \text{female}) = 0.45$
    \item $\bbP^*(D) = \hat{\bbP}(D)$ for the calculation of $\hat{h}^*(X)$
    \item $\bbP(D = \text{female} \mid X_{\text{smoker}} = \text{smoker}) = 0.8$
    \item $X_{\text{age}} \perp D$ and $X_{\text{age}} \perp X_{\text{smoker}}$
\end{itemize}

In the synthetic data experiment section, we use the identity transformation s.t. $T(X) = X$. We consider three pre-defined $\pi$'s (Definition \ref{def:pm}), namely $0.8$, $0.7$, and $0.6$ to asses how varying noise levels impact our method's performance (Theorem \ref{theorem:GEB}). Further, we also evaluate the performance of our method when there are errors in the noise rate estimation by considering a set of pre-defined estimation errors that yield $\hat{\pi} =\pi\pm 0.025$ and $\hat{\pi} = \pi \pm 0.05$ (Theorem \ref{theorem:GEB-NE}). We train our models using a simple 3-layer neural network with 5 neurons in each hidden layer using the MSE loss. 

To illustrate that 1) the ground truth is well-approximated when using both $X$ and $D$ (best-estimate model in Definition \ref{def:bep}), and 2) the unawareness premium (Definition \ref{def:up}) indeed introduces indirect discrimination. Below, we first present side-by-side figures that contain the ground truth, the best-estimate premium, and the unawareness premium:
