\section{Deferred Discussion on Assumptions}
\label{app:deferred_disc}

\subsection{Restrictions on Assumption A}

The restriction of Assumption A relies on the type of generator (which will influence the tail distribution of $\hat{\pi}$) and the number of data within each group (which will influence the accuracy of $\hat{\pi}$). The condition in Assumption A is equivalent to:
\[
\bbP \left( \frac{\left( 1 - \frac{1}{|\cD|} \right)^2}{t} > \left|\hat{\pi} - \frac{1}{|\cD|}\right| \right) \le \exp(\frac{-t}{K}),
\]
when $K > 0$ is a constant.

Generally speaking, this assumption holds if $\hat{\pi}$ is inverse exponential distributed
with a translation of $\frac{1}{|\cD|}$, or having a lighter tail than the inverse exponential distribution that is
\[
f_{\hat{\pi}}(t) \le \frac{1}{K(t-|\cD|)^2}\exp(-\frac{1}{K|t - \frac{1}{\cD}|}),
\]
when $t$ is close to $\frac{1}{|\cD|}$, where $f_{\hat{\pi}}(t)$ is the pdf of $\hat{\pi}$. Especially, since a bounded distribution is also sub-exponential, if $|\hat{\pi} - \frac{1}{|\cD|}| > \epsilon$, for some $\epsilon > 0$ condition is also satisfied. This will happen when the number of data within groups ($m$) is sufficiently large and $\pi - \frac{1}{|\cD|}$ is large enough.

\subsection{A General Discussion}

It is imperative to note that the support of our assumptions and theorem requires the availability of multiple independent datasets. when the observations are naturally organized in this manner, the application of the following assumptions is direct. Notice that the same concept applies to non-independent datasets with a mixing property (such as $\alpha$-mixing). In such cases, we only need to use the Bernstein inequality under strong mixing conditions(\cite{bernstein-inequality},\cite{bernstein-moderate-deviations}). 

According to the form of $\hat{C}_{1,k},$ the tail of this estimator is equivalent to the distribution of $\hat{\pi}_k = \max_{i\in[m]}\hat{g}_k(X_{k,i}^*)$ near $\frac{1}{|\cD|}$. If $\hat{g}_k$ is a good estimator as well as $m$ is large enough, $\hat{\pi}_k$ will be concentrated near $\pi > \frac{1}{|\cD|},$ which is guaranteed by Lemma \ref{lemma:NE}. Especially when $\pi - \frac{1}{|\cD|}$ is relatively large, it is reasonable to expect that $\hat{\pi}_k$ has a sparse distribution near $\frac{1}{|\cD|}$, which implies that $\hat{C}_{1,k}$ has a sub-exponential tail (or even bounded).  For Assumption B \ref{assumption:B}, notice that within every group $k\in[n_1],$ $\hat{\pi}_k$ are estimators for $\pi$, and thus $\hat{C}_{1,k}$ are plug-in estimator for $C_1.$ Since $\hat{C}_{1,k}$ are i.i.d., it is reasonable to assume $\hat{C}_{1,k}$ are ``nearly" unbiased. 

