\subsection{\textbf{Lemma} \ref{lemma:NE} Proof}
\label{proof:lemmaNE}
\textbf{Lemma \ref{lemma:NE}} Consider $\epsilon$-LDP setting with $\pi \in (\frac{1}{|\cD|}, 1]$ and $\bar{\pi} \in [0, \frac{1}{|\cD|})$. For some transformation of $X$, denoted by $X^* = \tilde{T}(X)$, assume there exists at least one anchor point $X_{\text{anchor}}^*$ in the dataset s.t. $\bbP(D = j^*|X_{\text{anchor}}^*) = 1$ for some $j^* \in [|\cD|]$. Then $\pi = \bbP(S = j^* |X_{\text{anchor}}^*)$. Empirically, for a dataset with $n$ observation, let $\boldsymbol{\eta}_{j^*}^n(X^*) = \left( \hat{\bbP}(S = j^*|X_1^*), \ldots, \hat{\bbP}(S = j^*|X_n^*)\right)$, then $\hat{\pi} = \left\| \boldsymbol{\eta}_{j^*}^n(X^*) \right\|_{\infty}$.

\begin{proof}

Notice that $\pi \in (\frac{1}{|\cD|},1]$, $\bar{\pi} \in [0, \frac{1}{|\cD|})$ and consequently we have $\pi > \bar{\pi}$. Hence, by Theorem 5 of \cite{pmlr-v139-zhang21k}, we are in a good position to apply the noise rate estimation method (Theorem 3) in \cite{patrini2017making} to estimate $\pi, \bar{\pi}$. Our $\epsilon$-LDP setting can be considered as a special case of CCN (class conditional noise) where the flip probability is the same across all groups in $\cD$. Consider
\[
\begin{aligned}
\bbP(S = j^* | X_{\text{anchor}}^*) &= \sum_{k=1}^{|\cD|} \bbP(S = j^*|D = k) \cdot \bbP(D = k | X_{\text{anchor}}^*) \\
&\overset{(a)}{=} \sum_{k=1}^{|\cD|} \bbP(S = j^* | D = k) \cdot \ones\{j^* = k\} \\ 
&= \pi,
\end{aligned}
\]
(a) is followed by the definition of anchor point
\[
\bbP(D = j^* | X_{\text{anchor}}^*) = 1 \implies \bbP(D = k | X_{\text{anchor}}^*) = 0, \forall k \ne j^*, k, j^* \in [|\cD|].
\]
Then one can easily see that $\bbP(S = j^* | X_i^*)$ attains its maximum when $\bbP(D = j^* | X_i^*) = 1$, since we know
\[
\begin{cases}
    \bbP(S = j^* | D = k) = \pi, \text{\ if $j^* = k$} \\
    \bbP(S = j^* | D = k) = \bar{\pi}, \text{\ if $j^* \ne k$}, \\
\end{cases}
\]
hence we know $\bbP(S = j^* | X_i^*)$ is actually a weighted sum of $\pi$ and $\bar{\pi}$, where the weights are simply $\{ \bbP(D = k | X_i^*) \}_{k=1}^{|\cD|}$. But we also know that $\pi > \bar{\pi}$. Hence, for empirical estimation, $\boldsymbol{\eta}_{j^*}^n = \left( \hat{\bbP}(S = j^*|X_1^*), \ldots, \hat{\bbP}(S = j^*|X_n^*)) \right)$, then $\hat{\pi} = \| \boldsymbol{\eta}_{j^*}^n \|_{\infty}$.

This completes the proof
\end{proof}

