However, certain methods show a degradation in accuracy for specific step sizes ($T/N$). For instance, with $N=400$, the MQ-RBF--RK3 (\textbf{B2(a)}) method exhibits an order $-1.4821$, followed by a return to the expected convergence rate. Similarly, the MQ-RBF--RK3 (\textbf{C1$+$}) and MQ-RBF--RK3 (\textbf{C2$+$}) methods initially demonstrate a consistent convergence rate of approximately $5$, which later degrades for $N=6400$. These temporary losses of convergence, observed in explicit methods with bounded stability regions, are likely due to the stiffness of the problem. Despite this, the global errors for these methods are lower than those produced by the corresponding classical RK methods. On a positive note, we also observe slight superconvergence in the MQ-RBF--RK3 (\textbf{C2$-$}) method for $N=6400$. Additionally, most of the $s$-stage MQ-RBF--RK methods yield a higher rate of convergence and better accuracy compared to the classical RK methods with $s+1$ stages.

