    We say that $L\in C^0(X)$ is a {\bf Lyapunov function} for $F$ if, for all $x\in X$, $y\in\cO_F^+(x)$ implies $L(x)\geq L(y)$, namely $L$ is non-increasing on each orbit.
    We say that $L$ is a {\bf strict Lyapunov function} for $F$ if $y\in\cO_F^+(x)$, $y\neq x$, implies $L(x)> L(y)$. 
    %the forward orbit of each point. for all $t\in\bR$, namely if $L$ is constant over each orbit $\cO_F(p)$.
    We denote by $\cL_F(X)$ the set of Lyapunov functions of $F$.
