We show below that being wandering is inherited by inverses, factors and products.
%
\begin{definition}
    A flow $G$ on a topological space $Y$ is a {\bf factor} of $F$ if there is a continuous, surjective and equivariant map $\phi:X\to Y$, namely $\phi(F^t(x))=G^t(\phi(x))$ for every $t\in\bR$.
    The flow $F$ is called an {\bf extension} of $G$.
\end{definition}
%
\begin{proposition}
    The following holds:
%    Let $F$ be a wandering flow on $X$. Then:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item The inverse of 
        a wandering flow 
        is wandering.
        \item Any extension of %$F$
        a wandering flow 
        is wandering.
        %if $F$ is an extension of a factor of $F$ is wandering, then $F$ is wandering;
        \item The product of wandering flows is wandering.
        %if $G$ is a wandering flow on $Y$, then the product flow $F\times G$ is wandering on $X\times Y$.  
    \end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}
