\begin{example}
   Consider the family of curves $\cF$ in Figure~\ref{fig: nr}.
   Outside of $s_\infty$, a consistent orientation can be given to each curve of $\cF$ so that each of them is the orbit of a flow.
   On the other side, any vertical line segment through any point of $s_\infty$ meets all curves $s_k$ for $k$ large enough.
   Hence, arbitrarily close to $s_\infty$, there are orbits where points move in opposite directions.
   This implies that $s_\infty$ cannot be the orbit of a flow: every semiflow $F$ having the curves in $\cF\setminus\{s_\infty\}$ as orbits, necessarily has each point of $s_\infty$ as a fixed point.
   In particular, $\cF$ is not the family of orbits of a regular flow.
\end{example}

