In Figure~\ref{fig:Bnd_MC_CPN} we show a representative example of the application of the bounding method to the MC autocorrelation function of the correlation length $\xi_\mathrm{G}$ (top panels) and the magnetic susceptibility $\chi_\mathrm{T}$ (bottom panels). Its error, depicted by shaded bands in Figure~\ref{fig:Bnd_MC_CPN} and used in the automatic procedure, is estimated according to Eq.~\eqref{eq:err_gamma}.
At first we focus on $\xi_\mathrm G$, neglecting the other measured observables (and their information on autocorrelations). We start by inspecting its lower bound, which provides an estimate of $\tau_\mathrm{int} \approx 4$ at $W=0$. Then we calculate the upper bound with a value of $\tau_0$ four times larger ($\tau_0 \approx 17$ as reported in orange in the top right panel of Figure~\ref{fig:Bnd_MC_CPN}) and find the optimal window from the saturation of the bounds: this gives us the first iteration of our algorithm which from this point on proceeds by re-calculating only the upper bound with $\tau_0$ set to (four times) the new estimate of $\tau_\mathrm{int}$. After a few iterations the window $W$ is stable and the iterative procedure stops leading to a summation window of $W=41$ (for comparison Wolff's $\Gamma$-method returns $W = 56$).
Similarly we apply the same adaptive bounding method to the observable with longest autocorrelation function observed, $\chi_\mathrm T$. Also in this case the lower bound is practically always flat hinting at a single mode dominance and the recursive procedure converges after a few iterations to a satisfactory window.
From the right panels of Figure~\ref{fig:Bnd_MC_CPN} we observe the expected behavior of the bounds and their saturation relative to their statistical errors. As it is evident from the plots, choosing as input for the upper bound four times the estimated $\tau_\mathrm{int}$ is rather conservative, and we adopted this procedure for illustrative purposes.

We repeat the same operation on the other observables and show the corresponding plots in Appendix~\ref{app:obs_cpn}, while their estimated errors and integrated autocorrelation times are reported in Table~\ref{tab:CPN_obs_tau} and are in good agreement with Ref.~\cite{Engel:2011re}. Notably we find that the lower bound is often quite flat.

As a consistency check, we repeated the analysis on all observables by setting $\tau_0$ equal to the largest integrated autocorrelation time observed, i.e. of $\chi_\mathrm T$, and found very good stability in our procedure. Depending on the available data, one may decide to choose either the iterative procedure or this approach.\\

