\begin{abstract}
% The emergence of open-source LLMs has created new opportunities for enterprises seeking to leverage this technology. However, many companies still lack the infrastructure to deploy and effectively serve large-scale LLMs. As a result, businesses that aim to deliver well-defined domain-specific services using small-scale LLMs (sLLMs) must address the performance limitations of these models. In this study, we propose Domain Adaptive Continual Pretraining (DACP), a methodology designed to enhance domain adaptation in sLLMs while minimizing knowledge degradation. DACP enables efficient specialization across domains and foundation models. We validate DACP’s effectiveness through experiments using general-domain benchmarks to assess knowledge forgetting and target-domain datasets to measure performance gains. Additionally, we demonstrate its applicability in real-world industrial services, showing that DACP-applied sLLMs outperform baseline models. These findings highlight DACP as a scalable and cost-efficient approach for optimizing sLLMs in commercial applications.

% The rise of open LLMs has expanded opportunities for enterprises, but many still lack the infrastructure to deploy and serve large-scale models, making small LLMs (sLLMs) a practical alternative despite their performance limitations. To address this, we propose Domain Adaptive Continual Pretraining (DACP), a methodology that enhances domain adaptation in sLLMs while mitigating knowledge degradation. We validate DACP through experiments using general-domain benchmarks to assess knowledge retention and target-domain benchmarks to measure performance improvements. Furthermore, real-world industrial applications demonstrate that DACP-applied sLLMs outperform baseline models, establishing DACP as a scalable and cost-efficient solution for optimizing sLLMs in industrial use.

The emergence of open-source large language models (LLMs) has expanded opportunities for enterprise applications; however, many organizations still lack the infrastructure to deploy and maintain large-scale models. As a result, small LLMs (sLLMs) have become a practical alternative, despite their inherent performance limitations. While Domain Adaptive Continual Pretraining (DACP) has been previously explored as a method for domain adaptation, its utility in commercial applications remains under-examined. In this study, we validate the effectiveness of applying a DACP-based recipe across diverse foundation models and service domains. Through extensive experiments and real-world evaluations, we demonstrate that DACP-applied sLLMs achieve substantial gains in target domain performance while preserving general capabilities, offering a cost-efficient and scalable solution for enterprise-level deployment.


\end{abstract}



