\section{Related Work}

% Large language models (LLMs) are being applied across various domains, with ongoing research exploring a wide array of tasks that leverage their embedded knowledge.(Kaur et al.,2024). As time passes, the knowledge embedded in a model gradually becomes obsolete, requiring resource-intensive retraining from scratch to ensure its applicability. (Jang et al.,2023). Retraining an LLM from scratch is prohibitively expensive and resource-intensive, which underscores the need for a more efficient methodology: continual learning(Wu et al.,2024). Instead of retraining on an entirely updated dataset, continual learning focuses on applying only newly added data to the existing model, a strategy that has been explored in various studies even before the advent of LLMs. (Ven et al.,2019 ; Parisi et al., 2019 )

Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly applied across various domains, with research exploring diverse tasks that leverage their embedded knowledge \cite{kaur2024texttransformationcomprehensivereview}. However, as models become outdated, retraining from scratch is prohibitively expensive, highlighting the need for continual learning, which updates models using newly added data rather than an entirely new dataset \cite{jang2023temporalwikilifelongbenchmarktraining,wu2024continuallearninglargelanguage,vandeven2019scenarioscontinuallearning,parisi2019continual}.

% Furthermore, the need for continual learning has been emphasized as a means to overcome the limitations of open models, which were initially developed with an English-centric focus(Wu et al.,2024). Early open models show relatively lower performance in languages other than English, highlighting the need for additional training to enhance performance in non-English languages (Zhao et al., 2024). With the advent of increasingly sophisticated open models, the language imbalance issue has been alleviated (Zhao et al., 2024 ; Cui et al., 2024 ; Kim et al., 2024), yet researchers have continued to focus on developing domain adaptation methods. (Ling et al.,2024 ; Zirui Song et al., 2025 ; Wei et al., 2024)

The need for continual learning has been emphasized to address the limitations of open models, which were initially developed with an English-centric focus and exhibited lower performance in other languages \cite{wu2024continuallearninglargelanguage,zhao2024largelanguagemodelshandle}. While advancements in open models have helped alleviate language imbalance \cite{zhao2024llamaenglishempiricalstudy,cui2024efficienteffectivetextencoding,kim2024efficienteffectivevocabularyexpansion}, researchers continue to explore domain adaptation methods to further enhance the model \cite{ling2024domainspecializationkeymake,song2025injectingdomainspecificknowledgelarge,lu2024finetuninglargelanguagemodels}.

% Such efforts have ranged from fine-tuning small-scale corpora to continual pretraining on medium-scale corpora, typically consisting of hundreds of gigabytes (Gupta et al., 2023). In particular, continual pretraining requires frequent updates of model parameters since the model must acquire additional knowledge from large volumes of data. This can lead to the loss of previously trained knowledge during the pretraining phase—a phenomenon known as catastrophic forgetting {Wang et al., 2024  }— which has been a long-standing challenge in continual learning research. In our study, we have explored various approaches to mitigate this issue and verified through experiments that general performance is maintained.

Efforts to enhance model adaptation have ranged from fine-tuning small-scale corpora to continual pretraining on medium-scale corpora, typically involving hundreds of gigabytes of data \cite{gupta2023continualpretraininglargelanguage}. However, continual pretraining requires frequent model updates, which can cause catastrophic forgetting, a persistent challenge in continual learning research \cite{parisi2019continual}, that we address through mitigation strategies and experimentally verify to maintain general performance.

% Moreover, from the perspective of continual pretraining, domain adaptation methods should be applied at the foundation model stage before instruction tuning \cite{gururangan2020dontstoppretrainingadapt}. Therefore, once DACP is complete, post-training is necessary to enhance the model’s instruction-following capabilities. Fortunately, research on this topic has also been actively conducted within the open-source community {REF}. In our study, after applying the proposed methodology, we observed that the instruction-following ability of the domain-adapted model is restored to the level of the original general domain model.

% From a continual pretraining perspective, domain adaptation should be applied to the foundation model before instruction tuning \cite{gururangan2020dontstoppretrainingadapt}, necessitating post-training to restore instruction-following capabilities after DACP. Our study confirms that after applying DACP, the instruction-following ability of the domain-adapted model returns to the level of the original general-domain model, aligning with ongoing efforts in the open-source community {REF}.

Our method should be applied to the foundation model before instruction tuning \cite{gururangan2020dontstoppretrainingadapt}, necessitating post-training to restore instruction-following capabilities. Fortunately, this topic has been actively studied in the open-source community \cite{sanh2021multitask}. %, and our experiments demonstrate that after applying DACP, the instruction-following ability of the domain-adapted model is restored to the level of the original general-domain model.

Unlike previous work \cite{jiang2024improving,wu2023bloomberggpt}, primarily focused on domain adaptation itself, we extend LLM domain adaptation research by verifying an adaptation recipe across various domains and backbone models, while also evaluating its effectiveness and industrial efficiency.


