\section{DACP Recipe}


% In industry, domain adaptation of large language models has traditionally been achieved through fine‑tuning on smaller domain-dataset(Ling et al., 2024). However, supervised fine‑tuning (SFT), conducted after the unsupervised pretraining of a foundation model, primarily focuses on teaching the model how to utilize the knowledge acquired during pretraining. {REF} In contrast, this study proposes to systematically inject domain knowledge into LLMs by leveraging mid‑scale domain corpora during the continual pretraining phase. Similar to how extensive pretraining on massive datasets enhances overall model performance, the substantial learning of domain data is expected to result in significant performance gains within the target domain. However, this approach requires frequent updates of model parameters to accommodate the large volume of data, which inherently causes catastrophic forgetting of knowledge obtained during the original pertaining.

In industry, domain adaptation of LLMs has traditionally relied on fine-tuning smaller domain datasets \cite{ling2024domainspecializationkeymake}. However, supervised fine-tuning (SFT) primarily utilizes pretrained knowledge rather than systematically incorporating new domain knowledge \cite{gekhman-etal-2024-does}. To address this, we propose leveraging mid-scale domain corpora during continual pretraining to enhance domain adaptation. However, this approach requires repeated updates to the weight parameters, which can lead to catastrophic forgetting of the pretrained knowledge.

% In the process of optimizing LLMs for domain adaptation, catastrophic forgetting is generally regarded as a phenomenon that must be mitigated (Li et al., 2017 ; Liao et al., 2024). In real-world service environments, user queries often extend beyond the target domain to encompass general topics. If a model becomes overly specialized in a particular domain and thereby relinquishes its general knowledge and reasoning capabilities, users may encounter a decline in response quality. Therefore, while domain adaptation should enhance expertise in the target domain, it must simultaneously preserve the model’s knowledge acquired during the pretraining phase. Achieving this balance ensures that the model maintains high performance within the target domain while generating reliable responses to diverse general queries, thereby ensuring consistent and comprehensive service.

% During the DACP process, training is conducted using a mid-scale corpus. A critical consideration in this process is the risk of catastrophic forgetting\cite{yogatama2019learning}, where the pretraining-acquired domain knowledge may be lost while learning from new domain data. This phenomenon can lead to an overall decline in model performance.

In LLM domain adaptation, catastrophic forgetting poses a significant challenge \cite{liu-etal-2024-catastrophic}, as excessive adaptation can degrade general knowledge and reasoning abilities, making it crucial to balance domain expertise with pretrained knowledge to maintain high performance in domain tasks and reliable responses to general queries \cite{li2017learningforgetting,liao2024exploringforgettinglargelanguage}, as illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:why_we_need_dacp}. 

During the DACP process, training is conducted using a mid-scale corpus. However, a key concern is the risk of catastrophic forgetting, where knowledge acquired during pretraining may be lost while learning new domain knowledge \cite{yogatama2019learning}. This phenomenon can lead to a decrease in overall model performance, underscoring the need for effective mitigation strategies.

% To mitigate this issue, our study applies a replay methodology (Scialom et al., 2022). This approach involves reusing a portion of the corpus that was originally employed during the foundation model's pretraining for further training in DACP, thereby minimizing catastrophic forgetting. However, since EXAONE (An et al., 2024) and most open-source LLMs (e.g., Llama, Qwen, etc.) (Grattafiori et al.,2024;Yang et al.,2025; Liu et al.,2025) do not explicitly provide their pretraining corpora, we estimated the composition of the general-domain datasets likely used during pretraining and constructed our own accordingly. This enables the model to continue next-token prediction based on its pretraining knowledge while simultaneously integrating new domain-specific information.

% To mitigate catastrophic forgetting, our study applies a replay methodology that reuses a portion of the corpus originally employed during the foundation model’s pretraining for further training in DACP \cite{scialom2022finetunedlanguagemodelscontinual}. However, since EXAONE \cite{research2024EXAONE3078binstruction} and most open-source LLMs (e.g., Llama, Qwen) do not explicitly disclose their pretraining corpora \cite{grattafiori2024llama3herdmodels,qwen2025qwen25technicalreport,deepseekai2025deepseekv3technicalreport}, we estimated the likely composition of their general-domain datasets and constructed a corresponding dataset. This approach allows the model to retain its pretraining knowledge through next-token prediction while simultaneously integrating new domain-specific information.

