\subsection{Post-training Dataset Preparation}

% DACP is not intended to develop instruction-following capabilities, but rather to acquire new domain knowledge. Therefore, we applied it to the base foundation model before instruction tuning. After applying DACP, the domain-adapted foundation model may show insufficient instruction following capability, which leads to subsequent post-training stages of instruction tuning and alignment tuning. Without a proper post-training process, even if the model has learned the domain knowledge, it may fail to use the knowledge effectively. Thus, both DACP and the post-training process play crucial roles in building a domain-adapted LLM.

% DACP is designed to acquire new domain knowledge, not to develop instruction-following capabilities, so it is applied to the base foundation model before instruction tuning. Thus, DACP-applied models may initially exhibit insufficient instruction-following ability; post-training stages, including instruction tuning and alignment tuning, are necessary to ensure effective knowledge utilization. Without proper post-training, even if a model has learned domain knowledge, it may fail to apply it effectively, making both DACP and post-training essential for building a domain-adapted LLM. To construct the post-training dataset, we utilized publicly available instruction-tuning datasets, including Tulu 3 \cite{lambert2025tulu3pushingfrontiers} and AIHub \cite{aihub2025}, along with a synthetic dataset generated from human-annotated seed data.

Since DACP focuses on domain knowledge acquisition rather than instruction-following, it is applied prior to instruction tuning. As a result, DACP-applied models may lack instruction-following ability, necessitating post-training (e.g., instruction and alignment tuning) to enable effective knowledge use. Without this step, models may fail to apply acquired domain knowledge. For post-training, we used public instruction datasets such as Tulu 3 \cite{lambert2025tulu3pushingfrontiers}, AIHub \cite{aihub2025}, and a synthetic dataset generated from human-annotated seed data.

