\section*{Appendix C: Data leakage avoidance}

\subsection*{Leakage}

\walter{Adding content about Leakage here. I still need to add finish it up and add references. Then we can decide the best place to put it, perhaps in the Data section?}

Leakage is generally defined as information that models are trained on that (a) is not available when predictions are made in the future on unseen data, and (b) make the model appear to be more performant than it actually is. In other words, leakage reduces the generalizability of a model, and can be very slight to very severe.

Leakage is, unfortunately, not uncommon in machine learning competitions. One might be tempted to think this is due to carelessness on the part of the challenge organizer. While this may be true on occasion, the hard truth is that leakage can be very subtle and difficult to detect.

This section is not intended to be a comprehensive discussion about leakage, as the topic is expansive and very context dependent. An analogy might be a discussion about bugs in software development; while there are general principles that can be discussed, there are orders of magnitude more details that depend on the programming language, the specific application, etc. Like finding bugs in the software development process, the more experience you have, the easier it is to spot common problems. It should be noted though, that, unlike bugs in software that can typically be patched without causing significant issues, leakage can completely spoil months of work preparing for a machine learning competition, often with no way to recover.

It is important to understand that participants in a challenge have motivations that extend beyond a pure desire to improve the state of the art on a particular problem. While most might be genuinely interested in advancing the field, adding cash prizes and/or the prestige of winning a competition can motivate others to attempt to gain any advantage they can, whether or not it actually helps solve the underlying problem. You should assume that at least some of the competition participants have a "hacker" mentality, in that they are more interested in the challenge of what they can get away with, rather than the actual problem of the competition. Assuming that all of the participants have the same motivations as the organizers can lead to serious disruption to the competition. 

\isabelle{Actually, I find that it depends on the community. The KDD community tends to want to win regardless the means, and will not disclose the leakage, if they find one. The ML community will generally react differently and disclose the leakage.}

While competition rules should be put in place to make it very clear to participants what is and isn't acceptable within the scope of the competition, organizers should not rely on the rules as the primary deterrent of cheating. There are many subtle and difficult-to-detect methods participants can use to incorporate leakage into their methods to gain an advantage [e.g., REF2]. The best way to avoid this is to remove leakage vectors before the competition is launched. One of the most important responsibilities of the challenge organizers is to ensure the data is free of leakage and think through and mitigate any other possible exploits. All competition organizers are encouraged to read REF1 when considering the construction of the challenge.

There are three general buckets of leakage that a challenge organizer should think about: access to ground truth, leakage intrinsic to the data, and leakage introduced when processing the data. These will each be discussed briefly, but it should be understood that it is impossible to list all of the ways leakage can occur. These are intended to help prompt thinking about possible leakage vectors, and should not be considered comprehensive guides.


\subsubsection*{Access to Ground Truth}

Nothing will ruin a competition (and all the hard work that went into it) faster than the participants gaining access to the test set ground truth. It seems obvious that these labels should not be accessible to participants for the duration of a machine learning competition. There are a number of ways this "obvious" statement can be subverted.

A common assumption is that it's acceptable for the ground truth of the test to be publicly accessible as long as it is difficult to find (e.g., the data is obfuscated or impractical to locate due to the volume of data). This assumption almost always leads to disappointment, as competitors can be very resourceful. While it may seem that obfuscating the data may be a robust solution, experience has shown that it often isn't. It should be assumed that any publicly available information will be found, and a competition that uses public data will always be at risk.

Steps should be taken to ensure the security of test data used for a competition before it launches. It should be stored in a location with limited access, and it should be known which people have access. (Individuals who have access and the groups tied to these individuals should generally be excluded from the competition.) Care should be taken that the test labels aren't published during the competition including, e.g., publishing graphs of the test ground truth [REF3] or otherwise describing or displaying the test label in a way that leaks information (e.g., in a previous youtube lecture [REF4]). This can happen when multiple groups have access to the data and signals get crossed. 

Steps should also be taken to ensure that credentials to access the data are not published openly (e.g., github). This seems like it should be obvious, but it has actually happened during at least one competition. It is far better to exercise caution in this regard and check for these issues in advance. 

\subsubsection*{Leakage Intrinsic to the Data}

It is a safe assumption that initial competition data has some sort of leakage, since there are so many ways it can manifest. It may help to consider a toy example. Let's assume your team is organizing a challenge to predict whether an image is of a cat or a dog. You are the data scientist in charge of converting the raw data into something useful for the competition. You are given two folders for the challenge, one folder containing all of the cats, and the other containing all of the dogs. What type of leakage should you consider? Here are a number of issues that might cause leakage.

The timestamps of the different images might leak information if the data was collected on different days, e.g., if the person collecting data for the challenge spent a week taking pictures of cats and then a week taking pictures of dogs, or if the person downloaded all of the cat images first and then the dogs. It's a best practice to always "randomize" (but in a repeatable deterministic manner) how files are saved for the competition.

If new photographs were taken specifically for the competition, individuals with different cameras may have preferentially taken images of cats or dogs, so that metadata indicating camera type (either by actual metadata with the image, or a proxy such as image resolution) could be predictive of the target, and therefore leakage.

\isabelle{Many people do not know that photographs have metadata incorporated in the format. We should tell them and maybe point them to an EXIF viewer \url{https://exif.tools/}.}

You generally want to strip files of any associated metadata (particularly with images), but that often isn't enough. A camera's model can often be determined from a raw image without using any embedded metadata, just through features such as resolution or even ways the jpeg is created from the camera sensor data. It is therefore important to standardize images so that the image (rather than its contents) leaks information. 

